<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Venice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Venetian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Venice is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> built on islands in a lagoon, which became a major maritime and commercial republic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Venetian merchants gained extensive trading privileges in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> from 1082, and Venice played a leading role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>'s sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204. It long dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s trade in Asian luxuries until the Portuguese opened the sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venice.jpg" alt="Venice" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kallerna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venezia_aerial_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
